                                                "sense_id": "1ff3b9a8-e5ec-38c7-9084-892dbc789884",
                                                "text": "Same-sex heterogender relationships are more openly acknowledged in Bugis society than same-sex homogender relationships."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "221560c2-09a6-46c3-9b0b-a5f98b799f69",
                                                "sense_id": "1ff3b9a8-e5ec-38c7-9084-892dbc789884",
                                                "text": "It was in the subtle sociolinguistic cues and gestures associated with Labrang Tibetan femininity I gradually took on in encounters, such as the way I came to talk and think about my \"husband\"–feeling extremely uncomfortable with public displays of heterogender affection, yet learning to participate in and expect the micropractices of bodily touch signifying homogender affection."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2738bcdb-5ab2-4514-9f78-42ae1390a842",
                                                "sense_id": "1ff3b9a8-e5ec-38c7-9084-892dbc789884",
                                                "text": "It is also quite likely, given the hegemonic status of marriage in Malay culture and throughout the entire Southeast Asian region both historically and at present, that most sexual relations between Malays at this time occurred in the context of marriage, which was invariably heterogender although not necessarily heterosexual."
                                            }
